  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Beautiful, mild, and unseasonably warm weather continues through\n  Friday. \n\n- Confidence continues to grow for a sharp transition to a colder\n  pattern with the potential of snow along the Continental Divide\n  this weekend.\n\nWarm and dry conditions remain over our region as high temperatures\ncontinue to run 15 to 20 degrees above normal. These conditions \nare about to change.\n\nFriday southerly breezes increase as a trough drops south out of\nCanada. Gusts up to 30 mph in valleys along with gusts to 50 mph\nin the mountains will be common. \n\nSaturday high temperatures will be up to 20 degrees cooler than\nFriday. Westerly winds gusting 15 to 25 mph may swing\nnortheasterly as a backdoor cold front is forecast. Sunday high\ntemperatures could be an additional 10 degrees cooler. Highs on \nFriday of mid to upper 70s becoming mid to upper 40s by Sunday. \nThe lowest valleys of North-central ID may remain near 60 but that\nwill also be close to a 30 degree drop compared to upper 80s on \nFriday.\n\nPrecipitation is still the biggest question mark. For instance,\nfrom the US-93 corridor and west the 48 hour total is 0.00 inches\nup to 0.50 inch. This means 25% of the models suggest \nprecipitation will remain along the Divide, while the 25% wetter \nmodels suggest up to 0.50 inch over most of western MT. \n\nThere is some confidence that along the Continental Divide and in\nsouthwest MT there could be 0.50 to 1.50 inches of precipitation\nby Monday morning. Couple that with snow levels lowering below \n4000 feet and area mountain passes could receive multiple wet, \nslushy inches of snow. Add extra time to your travels if crossing\nthe Divide on Sunday. Also if you have outdoor plans that take \nyou above 4000 feet this weekend, especially in and around Glacier\nNational Park, prepare for wet and raw conditions to avoid \nhypothermia.",
